Engineering Notes

Engineering Observations

SO-F-004 establishes the decision-generation stage within the current AVIA adaptive processing architecture.

Its primary responsibility is to transform interpreted operating context into a generated decision suitable for subsequent authorization and execution.

---

Engineering Considerations

Decision generation should remain sufficiently independent from perception, contextual interpretation, authorization, and physical execution to permit controlled evolution of each capability.

Decision generation may operate under incomplete, changing, or uncertain contextual information. Such conditions should be represented through appropriate engineering mechanisms rather than implicitly treated as certainty.

---

Known Limitations

This objective establishes the required decision-generation capability but does not define:

- Specific decision-generation algorithms.
- Artificial intelligence or machine-learning models.
- Optimization or planning techniques.
- Decision authorization mechanisms.
- Human authority mechanisms.
- Safety authorization mechanisms.
- Physical execution mechanisms.

These shall be established through derived objectives and associated engineering artifacts where required.

---

Authority Architecture Observation

A distinction has been identified between:

Generated Decision
        ↓
Authorization
        ↓
Approved Decision

The existence, ownership, and architectural form of the authorization mechanism remain unresolved.

This shall be investigated through subsequent architectural development rather than being artificially assigned to SO-F-004.

---

Future Considerations

Future decision-generation objectives may further define capabilities related to:

- Alternative response evaluation.
- Adaptive decision selection.
- Decision constraint evaluation.
- Decision confidence.
- Decision representation.
- Decision traceability.

Such capabilities shall remain traceable to SO-F-004 if formally derived.

---

Architectural Note

SO-F-004 currently represents an independent decision-generation boundary within the AVIA adaptive processing architecture.

The continued validity and appropriate placement of this boundary shall be evaluated through subsequent Engineering Review Gates as additional Functional, Safety, Human Authority, Coordination, and Governance objectives are developed.

No architectural pattern shall be promoted to a repository-wide standard solely on the basis of this objective.
